[Music]  i grew up a movie kid my parents had the  local theater in town so my sisters and  i spent a tremendous amount of time in  the theater i spent a lot of time in the  game room and in the arcade playing  games but also i really developed an  appreciation for what cinema was  and really it's much more than a picture  on screen it's an escape and i really  developed an appreciation for that  seeing so many people coming through the  theater  i just thought it would be great to  create kind of a one-stop shop  entertainment experience for everyone  that way they didn't have to go to  different places and they could go to a  single spot and then choose their  journey from there  i'm mitch roberts i'm the founder and  ceo of evo entertainment group we  combine all the different elements of  out of home entertainment so it's one  single venue that encompasses bowling  video games restaurant bar anchored by  the cinema  [Music]  i started in 2014 i was 18 at the time  and i didn't really know  what i was getting myself into i know  the movie business i didn't know much  other than that and so over  time  i think the biggest component of it all  is  surrounding myself with intelligent  talented people  that can teach me the things that i  don't know but also make the impact and  make the experience that much better in  ways that i couldn't do on my own  i didn't want to go bring on a bunch of  traditional cinema guys because that's  not the only thing we were doing  i wanted people from all aspects of  hospitality entertainment anything that  was attendance driven or experience  based one thing that my grandfather  always taught me was if you're the  smartest person in the room you're in  the wrong room  [Music]  the early days were a lot of fun it was  being on the ground working in the  venues all the time talking to people  what they liked about the venues what  they didn't like about the venues so we  would take all that feedback and then  adjust so as you you visit our different  venues you see little changes along the  way you know there's no two venues that  are exactly alike and the reason for  that is  just being so involved and being a small  company that you can make those changes  i think as we've gotten larger  particularly in the last six months as  the growth has really picked up we've  had to get a little bit more streamlined  and focus on the efficiencies in the  business aspect of it a little bit more  than the creative side  but we're still heavily focused on the  creative side of it just because that  was the root of who we were in the  beginning now we just have to be a  little bit more  grown up if you will  we were coming off of two record years  in 2018 and 2019 consecutive record  years so we were feeling really good  going into 2020 and then of course the  pandemic hit which was a dramatic event  for restaurants bars cinemas all the  things that we do one thing that covid  did is it highlighted your weaknesses or  the places that you could get better so  we had to make a really quick pivot  there  we did that in a number of ways from  turning our parking lots into drive-in  theaters we pivoted to food delivery  from a mobile app  we made like pumpkin patches for  halloween we made a paintball course so  it's just this whole  culture that we built  of never giving up and constantly  pivoting and adapting to the challenges  we're faced with we're constantly  looking at ways to improve or different  things that we can do and that's what  led us to where we are today  [Music]  showbiz was our largest deal to date  they were a what we call a multitainment  brand where they they have cinema and  bowling as well and so when we're able  to complete that acquisition what it did  is it tripled the size of our company  now we're in 16 venues we have 148  screens across texas oklahoma florida  and wyoming so it was a tremendous  change and tremendous step forward for  our business and positions us as the  largest operator of the cinema  entertainment center concept in north  america  in five to ten years i hope that we're a  national brand first of all but also i  think that our concept if done right and  continues to go the way it's going can  be a positive change for cinema in  general people have prophesized the fall  of cinema for a hundred years it's every  advancement in technology every downturn  in the economy people say that it's  going to be the downfall of cinema but  it's never died i think if you provide  an experience a good quality experience  there will always be demand for that  there will always be demand for shared  experiences and shared emotions and  watching a story unfold on screen  it's just we have to consistently adapt  and improve and evolve the way that we  show those films on screen  similar to retail people say retail's  dead retail is not dead  bad retail is dead  movie theaters are not dead they're not  dying  bad movie theaters are dead or down  you

